        <!-- WORKING PAPER 1 -->

        <div class="paper">

            <div class="paper-title">
                After the Draft: The Lasting Effects of Male Conscription on Well-Being
            </div>

            <div class="authors">
                <strong>Chiara Notarangelo</strong>
            </div>

            <div class="status">
                R&amp;R at Economica
            </div>

            <div class="paper-actions">

                <div class="abstract-button"
                     onclick="toggleAbstract('abstract1', this)">
                    ABSTRACT
                </div>

            </div>

            <div id="abstract1" class="abstract">

                I study the effects of compulsory military service on mental health by
                exploiting the 2005 suspension of conscription in Italy. Using
                individual-level health survey data, I implement a regression discontinuity
                design around the birth-cohort cutoff separating the last conscripted cohort
                from the first fully exempted one. Men exposed to compulsory service report
                significantly worse psychological and mental health, are less likely to feel
                calm, and are more likely to smoke. These effects are robust across
                non-parametric, parametric, and discrete specifications.

                <br><br>

                I also provide evidence of relative comparison effects across adjacent
                cohorts, which may amplify—but do not fully account for—the observed
                discontinuities. I further document spillover effects within households:
                cohabiting relatives of exposed men exhibit lower psychological wellbeing
                and a higher propensity to smoke, consistent with stress transmission
                mechanisms.

                <br><br>

                Finally, using data on causes of death (2000–2021), a
                difference-in-differences design shows that suicide rates among young men
                aged 20–24 declined disproportionately in low-education provinces. Since
                university students could defer service, the reform equalized exposure
                between students and non-students. No effects emerge for armed forces
                personnel, women, or other age groups.

                <br><br>

                Overall, even short-term compulsory service in peacetime generated
                persistent adverse effects on mental health and family wellbeing.

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- WORKING PAPER 2 -->

        <div class="paper">

            <div class="paper-title">
                Advancing Social Life Cycle Assessment in Healthcare Waste Management through Complementary Methodologies: A Scoping Review
            </div>

            <div class="authors">
                <strong>Chiara Notarangelo</strong>
            </div>

            <div class="status">
                R&amp;R at Journal of Environmental Management
            </div>

            <div class="paper-actions">

                <div class="abstract-button"
                     onclick="toggleAbstract('abstract2', this)">
                    ABSTRACT
                </div>

            </div>

            <div id="abstract2" class="abstract">

                Healthcare Waste Management (HCWM) represents a critical intersection of
                environmental, economic, and social challenges, particularly given the
                sector's exponential growth during and after the COVID-19 pandemic. Despite
                significant advancements in assessing environmental and economic dimensions,
                the social implications of HCWM remain under-explored.

                <br><br>

                Social Life Cycle Assessment (SLCA) has emerged as a complementary tool to
                address these gaps, yet its application in HCWM is limited by methodological
                constraints and under-representation in the literature. This scoping review,
                guided by the PRISMA-ScR protocol, aims to bridge this gap by investigating
                how complementary methodologies can enhance SLCA's application in HCWM
                through the analysis of 88 peer-reviewed studies.

                <br><br>

                The findings reveal significant variations in the focus on different SLCA
                subcategories. Subcategories such as Workers’ Health and Safety and Social
                Benefits/Social Security are frequently analysed, reflecting their relevance
                across diverse contexts. However, other critical social subcategories,
                including child labour and corruption, are either neglected or barely
                addressed, underscoring gaps in the current body of literature.

                <br><br>

                The review highlights the need for tailored SLCA frameworks that incorporate
                complementary methodologies, regional contexts, and active stakeholder
                engagement to bridge these gaps and improve the comprehensiveness of social
                impact assessments in HCWM. By advancing SLCA practices and aligning them
                with global sustainability goals, this study provides actionable insights
                for policymakers and researchers aiming to develop sustainable and equitable
                HCWM systems.

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- WORKING PAPER 3 -->

        <div class="paper">

            <div class="paper-title">
                I Don’t Care. Engaging Adolescents for Digital Health Tools Improvement
            </div>

            <div class="authors">
                <strong>Chiara Notarangelo</strong>, Moschetti, F. Quattrone,
                S. Nuti, Pennucci, F.
            </div>

            <div class="status">
                Submitted
            </div>

            <div class="paper-actions">

                <div class="abstract-button"
                     onclick="toggleAbstract('abstract3', this)">
                    ABSTRACT
                </div>

            </div>

            <div id="abstract3" class="abstract">

                Digital Health Tools (DHT) are increasingly recognised as key instruments
                for improving healthcare access, prevention, and patient engagement.
                However, adolescents remain underrepresented in the adoption of
                institutional digital health platforms, and little is known about the
                factors shaping their engagement.

                <br><br>

                This study investigates adolescents’ awareness, expectations, and
                perceptions of DHT through a pilot intervention involving 181 high-school
                students from inner areas of Tuscany, Italy. Participants first completed
                a baseline questionnaire assessing digital health literacy and attitudes
                toward DHT. They were then invited to participate in an incentive-based
                challenge designed to encourage use of the Italian Electronic Health Record
                (<em>Fascicolo Sanitario Elettronico</em>, FSE), followed by a comparative
                evaluation of the FSE and a WebApp, co-designed with adolescents, using the
                Mobile App Rating Scale (MARS).

                <br><br>

                Baseline findings reveal limited familiarity with institutional DHT despite
                generally positive attitudes toward digital technologies and high
                expectations regarding usability, accessibility, and privacy. Notably, the
                incentive-based intervention failed to generate any participation,
                suggesting that external rewards alone are insufficient to overcome
                motivational and administrative barriers.

                <br><br>

                Comparative evaluations indicate complementary strengths: the WebApp was
                perceived as more engaging, aesthetically appealing, and easier to use,
                whereas the FSE scored higher in credibility and informational authority.
                The findings suggest that successful adolescent engagement with digital
                health requires the integration of participatory design, institutional
                trust, reduced administrative burden, and interventions aimed at
                strengthening digital health literacy.

            </div>

        </div>


        <!-- WORKING PAPER 4 -->

        <div class="paper">

            <div class="paper-title">
                “Beeyond” Willingness To Pay: On the Perception of Honeybees’ Worth in the Italian Landscape
            </div>

            <div class="authors">
                <strong>Chiara Notarangelo</strong>, A. Carrozzo Magli
            </div>

            <div class="status">
                Submitted
            </div>

            <div class="paper-actions">

                <div class="abstract-button"
                     onclick="toggleAbstract('abstract4', this)">
                    ABSTRACT
                </div>

            </div>

            <div id="abstract4" class="abstract">

                Employing a game-theoretic model, we find that agents’ Willingness To Pay
                (WTP) to preserve an environmental service diminishes as their perceived
                expected environmental damage increases, even though it rises with their
                wealth and altruistic tendencies.

                <br><br>

                This result contradicts standard predictions, which rely on two key
                assumptions: the absence of strategic interactions and a binary choice
                between paying to completely avoid environmental loss or bear its
                consequences.

                <br><br>

                To empirically validate our theoretical predictions, we conducted an
                original survey assessing the valuation of Italian honeybees, a critical
                bioindicator recently impacted by climate change and invasive species.
                Additionally, we leveraged data from the European Social Survey to reinforce
                the robustness of our findings, instrumenting the perceived risk of future
                environmental hazards with the interaction between historical variations
                in average temperatures since the 1960s and individuals’ beliefs about the
                risk of power cuts.

                <br><br>

                Our results challenge previous hypotheses and findings on the relationship
                between environmental awareness, risk perception, and WTP for environmental
                preservation, with important implications for sustainability initiatives
                based on the paradigm of ecological transition.

            </div>

        </div>

